Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 05:40:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] camera: choose when the flash fires

Snapshot has no flash control at all, so a phone with a camera flash
carries a lamp its camera app cannot switch on. Add the three modes a
camera app is expected to offer - off, automatic, always - as a menu
button beside the countdown one, backed by a setting so the choice
survives a restart.

The flash is driven as a torch through the kernel's LED flash class,
found by looking for the flash class's own attributes rather than by
name, so this is not tied to one device: a machine with no flash, or one
whose LED is not writable, shows no button instead of a button that
does nothing.

Two things are deliberate. The light comes on 600 ms before the capture
rather than with it, because the exposure loop has to be given time to
answer it - a torch lit at the instant the shutter runs is metered as
though it were not there. And the automatic mode reads the ambient light
sensor rather than the viewfinder's own brightness: automatic exposure
holds that near mid-grey whether the room is lit or not, so measuring it
would measure the exposure loop. The signal that would really describe
the frame is the exposure time and gain the algorithm settled on, and
libcamera does not publish those back through PipeWire.

The countdown slot in the layouts becomes a two-button box and is
renamed accordingly, so all four layouts place both buttons without
each having to name them.
